     "type": "text",
     "text": "{\"count\": 2, \"rationale\": \"No single control is enough. Phishing is always available, so without segmentation the attacker always reaches the internal network. With segmentation alone, the unpatched workstation can still be escalated to pivot. With mfa:admin alone, the attacker reaches ciserver, escalates it using the kerberoasted admin credential, leaks dbadmin credentials, and authenticates to db. With mfa:dbadmin alone, the attacker uses the stolen admin credential on dc/adfs, gets admin there, and bypasses dbadmin MFA. No patch blocks every path. Two controls do work: mfa:admin plus mfa:dbadmin. mfa:admin blocks auth_dc and auth_adfs, so admin_dc and admin_adfs are unreachable. Then mfa:dbadmin blocks auth_db, auth_fileserver and auth_backup. That removes every foothold needed for exfiltration.\"}"
    }
   ],
